A whole program in which the Subject reaches a function only through a
store through reflection that the upward pass does not follow, and then
through a call inside package fmt: fmt.Sprint calls the Error method of
the value that the store wrote, and the method keeps its receiver. No
other call of the program brings a value of that type to a call of an
Error method, so the call graph of the program's own instructions does
not have the method at the call inside package fmt. The flooding pass
must reach it all the same: a Membership Query answers "no, apart from
reflection", never a plain "no". The program is on its own: next to the
cases of sound_calls.txtar the flooding pass reaches the function
without the wider call edges, and the case would pin nothing. The program
prints what errors.Is says when it runs.
-- go.mod --
module example.com/x

go 1.24
-- main.go --
package main

import (
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"reflect"
)

type P struct{ n int }

var last error

func (p *P) Error() string { last = p; return "p" }

var ErrS1 error = &P{1}
var dests []reflect.Value

func store() { dests[0].Set(reflect.ValueOf(ErrS1)) }

func SprintValue() error {
	v := reflect.New(reflect.TypeOf((*error)(nil)).Elem()).Elem()
	dests = append(dests, v)
	store()
	_ = fmt.Sprint(v.Interface())
	return last
}

func main() {
	fmt.Println("SprintValue", errors.Is(SprintValue(), ErrS1))
}
